   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Random Forest Classifier: Cross-Validation, Tuning, Final Fit, Feature Importance\n",
    "\n",
    "We do this in four sub-steps, each addressing a specific weakness of a \"just fit one model and report one accuracy number\" approach:\n",
    "\n",
    "**6a. Cross-validation on the training set** — a single train/test split is one snapshot; if we got a lucky or unlucky split, the accuracy number could be misleading. 5-fold stratified cross-validation gives a mean accuracy *and* a spread, so we know how stable the model's performance actually is.\n",
    "\n",
    "**6b. A small hyperparameter search** — rather than picking `n_estimators=300` arbitrarily, we compare a few reasonable configurations using cross-validation, and only then commit to one.\n",
    "\n",
    "**6c. Fit the chosen model on the full training set, evaluate once on the held-out test set** — the test set is touched exactly once, at the end, to avoid tuning-induced leakage.\n",
    "\n",
    "**6d. Permutation importance, not just Gini importance** — Gini-based feature importance is known to be biased toward continuous, high-cardinality features, which describes literally all 77 of our proteins. Permutation importance — how much accuracy drops when a feature's values are randomly shuffled — is a more trustworthy, model-agnostic alternative.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "83a399b7",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
