  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "47cce833",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Is this overlap actually meaningful, or could it happen by chance?\n",
    "\n",
    "Picking 15 \"important\" proteins out of 77 at random, purely by chance, would sometimes overlap with any given list of 10 \"candidate\" proteins just by luck. We can quantify exactly how likely our observed overlap is under pure chance using the **hypergeometric distribution** — the correct tool for \"how many matches when drawing without replacement from a fixed population,\" which is exactly this situation (77 proteins total, 10 EDA candidates, 15 RF top features).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "899c393a",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-07-17T05:54:02.456708Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-07-17T05:54:02.455993Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-07-17T05:54:02.462274Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-07-17T05:54:02.461535Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Population size (total proteins): 77\n",
      "EDA candidates: 10, RF top features drawn: 15\n",
      "Observed overlap: 3\n",
      "P(overlap >= 3 by chance alone) = 0.3010\n",
      "\n",
      "=> The overlap is NOT statistically distinguishable from what pure chance would produce (p >= 0.05) -- worth stating honestly rather than overselling the agreement between methods.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from scipy.stats import hypergeom\n",
    "\n",
    "M = len(protein_cols)      # population size: 77 proteins total\n",
    "n = len(eda_top10)         # number of EDA candidates (the population's exact successes we're targeting)\n",
    "N = 15                     # number of Random Forest top features drawn\n",
    "k_observed = len(overlap)  # observed overlap\n",
    "\n",
    "# P(X >= k_observed) -- probability of seeing this much overlap or more, by pure chance\n",
    "p_value_overlap = hypergeom.sf(k_observed - 1, M, n, N)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Population size (total proteins): {M}\")\n",
    "print(f\"EDA candidates: {n}, RF top features drawn: {N}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Observed overlap: {k_observed}\")\n",
    "print(f\"P(overlap >= {k_observed} by chance alone) = {p_value_overlap:.4f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "if p_value_overlap < 0.05:\n",
    "    print(\"\\n=> The overlap is unlikely to be due to chance (p < 0.05) -- \"\n",
    "          \"genuine evidence the two independent methods agree.\")\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(\"\\n=> The overlap is NOT statistically distinguishable from what pure chance \"\n",
    "          \"would produce (p >= 0.05) -- worth stating honestly rather than overselling \"\n",
    "          \"the agreement between methods.\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5863af91",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation:** This is the honest version of last time's claim. The observed overlap of 3 proteins (`pPKCG_N`, `P38_N`, `NR1_N`) between the EDA's top 10 candidates and the Random Forest's top 15 gives p = 0.30 — well above the p < 0.05 threshold. That means this overlap is **not** statistically distinguishable from what pure chance would produce. It's still a nice anecdote — `pPKCG_N` in particular shows up as a strong signal in both the EDA's statistical test and the model's feature importance — but the overlap as a whole shouldn't be leaned on as strong evidence that the two methods independently converge on the same biology.\n"
