    "**Interpretation:** This run's classification report is a clean sweep — every one of the 8 classes hits precision, recall, and f1-score of 1.00 on the 216-mouse test set, so there's no single class the model struggles with here. That said, this is worth treating with a healthy dose of skepticism rather than pure celebration: in earlier runs (and potentially on different sklearn versions) this same code produced 215/216 correct, with the one error being `t-CS-m` mistaken for `c-CS-s` — the two classes that share the same Behavior label and differ only in Treatment, which is exactly the kind of subtle distinction a model could occasionally blur. That the confusion (when it appears at all) is between two closely related classes, rather than something biologically nonsensical, is itself a reassuring sign — and it lines up with the EDA's PCA plot, where Genotype/Treatment groups showed partial rather than clean separation. The honest takeaway is: this model is consistently in the 99.5–100% test-accuracy range across runs, with cross-validated accuracy (98.6% ± 0.7%) the more stable number to quote, since it isn't sensitive to which single split or library version happened to run.\n"
