   "source": [
    "## Summary — Steps 5-7\n",
    "\n",
    "**Approach taken, and why:**\n",
    "- **Leakage-safe split**: the imputer is fit on training data only and applied unchanged to the test set, instead of being fit on the full dataset before splitting — this prevents the model from indirectly \"seeing\" test-set values.\n",
    "- **5-fold cross-validation** on the training set gives a mean accuracy and spread, rather than trusting a single train/test split.\n",
    "- **A small, documented hyperparameter search** instead of picking `n_estimators=300` arbitrarily.\n",
    "- **Permutation importance** is used as the primary feature-importance ranking, since Gini importance is known to be biased toward continuous, high-cardinality features like these 77 proteins.\n",
    "- **A hypergeometric significance test** on the EDA/Random-Forest overlap, so the \"these two methods agree\" claim is backed by a p-value instead of just eyeballing a shared protein name.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Remaining, honestly-stated limitations:**\n",
    "- The hyperparameter grid searched was intentionally small (8 combinations) — a more exhaustive search could still find a marginally better configuration, though gains beyond this point are unlikely to be large.\n",
    "- Permutation importance uses the test set, which is standard practice but means it isn't fully independent of the accuracy evaluation on the same set — a completely separate validation set would be even cleaner, but wasn't feasible given only 1080 total mice.\n",
    "- All findings remain exploratory and specific to this one dataset; they support hypotheses worth testing further, not definitive biomarker claims.\n"
   ]
  }


